---

<GuidePage title='Server-Side Rendering - App Router Guide' root='..'>
  <div class='kicker'>Chapter 4 · Part A: Maximize the Known</div>
  <h1>Server-Side Rendering</h1>
  <p class='lede'>
    Part A is about maximizing the known, and server-side rendering is the machine that makes the
    known possible at all. This chapter is the why behind every technique that follows: what SSR is,
    what it buys, how it changes what "fast" means, and how routing actually moves content around
    once the server is doing the rendering.
  </p>

  <h2 id='what'>4.1 What server-side rendering is</h2>
  <p>
    Server-side rendering means exactly what it says: your React components execute
    <strong>on the server</strong>, per request, and what travels to the browser is their
    <em>output</em>, real HTML, rather than the JavaScript machinery needed to produce that output.
    The browser can paint that HTML immediately, before any bundle downloads, parses, or runs.
    Compare the browser-only model, where the server sends an empty shell and the page earns every
    pixel through a boot sequence: download JS → execute → mount → fetch → render.
  </p>
  <p>
    In the App Router this is not a feature you enable; it is the default posture of the entire
    framework. <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='critical' /> Every component is a server component unless you mark it otherwise, so every
    route starts life fully server-rendered. Even client components participate: Next.js renders their
    initial HTML on the server too, then <em>hydrates</em> them (attaches the event handlers and state)
    once their JavaScript arrives. The practical consequence, and the reason this chapter leads Part A:
    <strong>everything the server can resolve arrives finished</strong>, and only the parts you
    explicitly carve out ever depend on the client. The rest of Part A is about keeping that
    carved-out surface small (<a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html'>§5</a>–<a
      href='09-server-resolvable-state.html'>§9</a
    >); Part B is about handling what remains (<a href='10-containers-content-shifting.html'>§10</a
    >–<a href='13-blocking-requests.html'>§13</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id='benefits'>4.2 What you get for it</h2>
  <p>
    The
    <a
      href='https://nextjs.org/docs/14/app/building-your-application/rendering/server-components'
      target='_blank'
      rel='noopener'>Next.js docs</a
    >
    enumerate the benefits of server rendering at length; collapsed, they come down to these:
  </p>
  <table class='tbl'>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th style='width: 32%'>Benefit</th>
        <th>Why it matters</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Data fetching at the source</strong></td>
        <td>
          Fetches run on the server, close to the database and internal services: fewer, faster
          round trips, instead of every widget dialing home from the browser across the public
          internet.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Security by locality</strong></td>
        <td>
          Tokens, API keys, and privileged queries <strong>never leave the server</strong>. The
          browser receives rendered results, not the credentials and endpoints that produced them.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Less JavaScript shipped</strong></td>
        <td>
          Server components add <strong>zero bytes to the bundle</strong>: their code, their
          dependencies, their markdown parser, all of it stays behind. The client downloads markup,
          not the machinery (<a href='07-first-render-js.html'>§7</a>).
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>Rendered once, served many</strong></td>
        <td>
          Rendered output is an artifact the framework can
          <strong>cache and reuse across users</strong>; see
          <a href='#page-cache'>§4.4</a> and <a href='15-dedupe-caching.html'>§15</a>.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>First paint and SEO</strong></td>
        <td>
          Real HTML on the first response means
          <strong>content is visible before JavaScript runs</strong>: for users, for crawlers, and
          for link unfurlers alike.
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Those are real, and they're the ones you'll see cited everywhere. But two benefits matter more
    to this guide than all of the above, because they are the ones traditional React habits give
    away first: <strong>stability</strong> and
    <strong>simplicity</strong>.
  </p>

  <h3 id='stability'>Stability: the known arrives first, already placed</h3>
  <p>
    Content shifting happens when content arrives <em>late</em> and pushes its neighbors around (<a
      href='10-containers-content-shifting.html'>§10</a
    > is devoted to it). SSR attacks the problem at its root: whatever the server resolves is part of
    the
    <em>first paint</em>, already in its final position, at its final size. It cannot shift
    anything, because it never "arrives"; it was simply there.
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='critical' />
  </p>
  <p>
    This is the direct, physical link between SSR and maximizing the known:
    <strong
      >every piece of content you move from client fetching to server rendering is a piece of
      content that can no longer displace the page</strong
    >. The layout-shift surface of a page is, almost by definition, its unknown surface. Shrink the
    unknown and the shifting shrinks with it; what little remains gets contained inside reserved
    containers (<a href='10-containers-content-shifting.html#reserving'>§10.3</a>) instead of being
    fixed after the fact.
  </p>

  <h3 id='simplicity'>Simplicity: content that doesn't depend on timing</h3>
  <p>
    Here is the elaboration this deserves, because "SSR is simpler" sounds backwards to anyone who
    learned React in the browser, and it is the single biggest quality-of-life difference in
    practice.
  </p>
  <p>
    On the server, an <code>async</code> component <code>await</code>s its data and renders it.
    That's the whole story. Between the await and the render there is
    <strong>no moment where the component exists but its data doesn't</strong>, so an entire
    category of questions evaporates: What do I render while it's loading? Where does the loading
    flag live? What if the response lands after the user changed filters? What's the fallback if the
    data isn't there <em>yet</em>, as opposed to not there <em>at all</em>? None of these are
    answerable "wrong" on the server. They are <em>unaskable</em>. Content stops depending on the
    timing of requests and responses, so the code stops modeling time.
  </p>
  <p>
    Client-side, every one of those questions must be answered
    <strong>at every layer that fetches</strong>, and the answers stack. A component can't fetch
    until it mounts, and it can't mount until its parent's fetch resolves and renders it. So the
    requests serialize down the tree (render → mount → effect → fetch → children mount → <em
      >their</em
    > fetch…), and each layer of the DOM grows its own loading state while it waits on the layer above.
    The visible result is the most recognizable symptom in the Tracker codebase: <strong
      >spinners rendered inside spinners</strong
    >, loading UI at three depths of the same page, each replaced at a different moment as its
    request lands.
  </p>

  <Diagram>
