    <Fragment slot='caption'>
      The spinner tower. Client-side, each DOM layer is blocked by the client-side request above it
      (the page can't fetch until it hydrates, the section can't fetch until the page renders it,
      the widget can't fetch until the section does), so loading states appear at every depth and
      resolve at different moments. Server-side, the same tree renders in one pass: each <code
        >await</code
      > is just a value, and the browser receives the finished result.
    </Fragment>
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    Notice what the right side <em>doesn't have</em>: loading flags, fallback branches, effect
    dependencies, race-condition guards, "is it undefined because it's loading or because it's
    empty" ambiguity (<a href='11-suspense-streaming.html#orthogonal'>§11.6</a>). That code was
    never simplified; it was never written. Multiply that by every data-bearing component on every
    page, and "simplicity" stops being an abstract virtue and becomes the difference between a
    codebase you can reason about and one you can't.
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='very' />
  </p>
  <p>
    There is a second, quieter reason the server side is simpler:
    <strong>the environment itself.</strong> Server code runs in one place you own and provisioned. It
    executes serially, you know what has resolved at every step, and the machine, the runtime, and the
    network path to the database are the same on every request. Client code runs on <em
      >someone else's computer</em
    >, and everything about that computer is an input to your correctness: their connection speed,
    their browser and its version, their CPU (which changes the <em>timing</em> of everything), their
    corporate firewall, and their ad blocker, which routinely eats entire requests that your code assumed
    would resolve. On the server none of those variables exist. On the client every one of them is a bug
    report you haven't received yet.
  </p>
  <p>
    And for every piece of content it owns, client rendering forces a choice that server rendering
    never asks: <strong>guess, or hide.</strong> Either you render fallback values while the data loads
    (skeletons and placeholder text that have to approximate the real content's size, and be maintained
    forever as the real content evolves: how tall is a profile page's bio you haven't loaded yet? guess
    wrong and the page jumps when it lands), or you reveal nothing until everything is ready (and the
    user stares at a blank region while content that was knowable sits hidden behind it). Both answers
    are ongoing work; each component that fetches must pick one and keep it correct. On the server, for
    everything resolvable at request time, the question is unaskable: the content arrives already the
    size it is (<a href='10-containers-content-shifting.html'>§10</a> picks this up for the parts that
    genuinely must wait).
  </p>
  <Callout kind='voice' title='"But client-side rendering is simpler"'>
    <p>
      It seems simpler exactly as long as nobody is sweating the details. If janky loading, layout
      shift, skeletons drifting out of sync with the content they stand in for, and regions
      resolving in whatever order the network picked are all acceptable, then yes: fetch in an
      effect and ship it. The moment the bar becomes an application that looks and feels
      professional (loads in a predictable sequence, holds still, handles every error and loading
      state cleanly), the client-side version of that is not simpler. It is dramatically more
      complex, and past a certain surface area it stops being achievable at all. Tracker is not a
      demo with a handful of forgiving users; things shouldn't flash and shift around, and loading
      should be something the user can predict and understand. That is the bar, and server-first is
      the cheaper way to clear it.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h3 id='output-not-machinery'>The output crosses the wire, not the machinery</h3>
  <p>
    Two rows of the benefits table deserve to be read together, because they are one fact seen from
    two sides: <strong
      >the only thing that ever crosses the network boundary is the rendered output.</strong
    > Think about what it takes to produce a reports table: the component code that lays it out, the data-layer
    function that queries for rows, the connection string and API tokens that query needs, the formatting
    and charting libraries that turn numbers into markup. In a client-rendered architecture, most of that
    ships to the browser, because the browser is where the rendering happens. In a server-rendered one,
    none of it does. The client receives what those things <em>produced</em>: finished markup.
  </p>

