  <p>
    This is why "security by locality" and "less JavaScript shipped" are the same row twice.
    Security: the credentials, the privileged queries, and the internal API shapes cannot leak to
    the browser because they were never sent, and
    <code>server-only</code> (<a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#third-party'>§5.5</a>) turns
    any accidental attempt into a build error. Performance: those rendering libraries are precisely
    the heavyweight imports of
    <a href='08-bundle-size-code-splitting.html'>§8</a>, and keeping them on the server is the
    single cheapest bundle-size win available, because the client downloads a few kilobytes of
    markup instead of megabytes of machinery (<a href='07-first-render-js.html'>§7</a>). One
    architectural fact, two benefits: expose the output, never the means of producing it.
  </p>

  <h3 id='api-is-html'>The API is your HTML</h3>
  <p>
    Push that one step further and it changes what "API" means for a page.
    <strong>With Next.js, the API is your HTML.</strong>
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='very' /> The contract between the server and the browser is not "here are the rows, render
    them yourself"; it is the rendered page. On first load that is literal HTML; on navigation it is the
    RSC payload (<a href='#routing'>§4.5</a>), which is still <em>rendered output</em>, a
    description of finished UI, never the raw data that produced it. The server returns what the
    data <em>became</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Sit with what that deletes. In the fetch-and-render world, every page needs a second, parallel
    product: a JSON endpoint shaped for it, versioned with it, kept in sync with it, plus the
    client-side code that fetches, validates, and maps that JSON back into markup. That is an API
    you have to design, document, and maintain, and its only consumer is your own page. Server-first
    collapses the two products into one: the unit the server exposes is not
    <code>/api/reports</code> returning rows, it is the reports page returning the table. The data's shape
    can change freely because nothing in the browser ever depended on it. (Mutations get the same collapse
    from server actions, <a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#server-actions'>§5.8</a>: a typed
    call instead of a hand-built endpoint. Building JSON routes for your own pages' first-render
    data is the pattern the audit flagged, <a href='18-applied-to-tracker.html'>§18</a>.)
  </p>
  <p>
    And there is a name for where this is heading. The pattern emerging across the ecosystem is that <strong
      >the data layer is your server components</strong
    >: rather than a data API that returns raw JSON for callers to render (client-side, or even
    server-side in a second pass), teams increasingly encapsulate direct server data access <em
      >together with</em
    > the UI that presents it, and expose those pieces as server-rendered components: microfrontends whose
    contract is finished, typed UI. The HTTP data layer stops being the default integration point between
    products; the component is, and the middleman surface it used to drag in (<a
      href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#server-actions'>§5.10</a
    >) never comes into existence. §5.10 also carries the honest caveat: the moment a consumer isn't
    React, the raw-data API earns its place back.
  </p>

  <h2 id='perceived'>4.3 Perceived performance, and where streaming fits</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Perceived performance</strong> is how fast a page <em>feels</em>, which is not the same
    thing as how long it takes to finish. Users don't experience totals; they experience milestones:
    when something first appears, when the content they came for is readable, whether things move
    around while they wait, and when the page responds to input. A page that paints meaningful
    content in 300ms and quietly finishes a slow widget at 3s <em>feels fast</em>. A page that shows
    a spinner until everything is ready at 1.5s feels slower, despite technically "finishing"
    sooner.
  </p>

  <Callout kind='info' title='The Next.js docs, on why this matters'>
    <p>
      "SSR with React and Next.js helps improve the perceived loading performance by showing a
      non-interactive page to the user as soon as possible." (<a
        href='https://nextjs.org/docs/14/app/building-your-application/rendering/server-components'
        target='_blank'
        rel='noopener'>Server Components, Next.js docs</a
      >)
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <p>
    Read that carefully: <em>non-interactive</em>, <em>as soon as possible</em>. The docs are
    conceding something and claiming something. The concession: the first paint isn't interactive
    yet: hydration hasn't happened. The claim: that trade is worth it, because seeing real content
    immediately dominates the user's perception of speed. People spend the first moments on a page <em
      >reading</em
    >, not clicking; SSR uses that window to be useful while the JavaScript catches up.
  </p>
  <h3 id='streaming-principle'>Streaming: the same principle, inside one response</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Streaming is the same principle applied within a single response.</strong> Plain SSR has a
    weakness: if the server waits for <em>all</em> data before sending anything, the slowest query gates
    the entire page. Streaming removes that gate: the server sends the known immediately (structure, containers,
    everything already resolved) and flushes the slow parts into their reserved slots as they finish,
    over the same response (<a href='11-suspense-streaming.html#streaming'>§11.2</a>). Perceived
    performance is governed by when the first meaningful content lands and whether the page holds
    still afterward; streaming makes the first independent of your slowest query, and containers (<a
      href='10-containers-content-shifting.html'>§10</a
    >) guarantee the second. That is the whole Part B playbook in one sentence, powered by the fact
    that rendering happens on a server that can flush incrementally.
  </p>

  <h3 id='tti'>Time-to-interactive is a dead metric</h3>
  <p>
    A question worth asking a room of engineers: how many of you actually interact with a page
    within 500ms of it loading? Nobody does. You land, you orient, you read; the first click comes
    seconds later, after the content has told you what is worth clicking on. Optimizing for the
    earliest possible moment of interactivity is optimizing a moment that nobody uses.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is why TTI is, for practical purposes, a dead metric. Everything in this architecture is
    about optimizing what the user can <em>see</em> and <em>read</em>: getting real content onto the
    page as fast as humanly possible. The interactive leaves hydrate some time later, and that is
    fine, because interactivity is not needed immediately and hydration is invisible. A button whose
    click handler attaches 800ms after first paint looks identical to one that was interactive from
    the first frame; the user cannot tell the difference, because they were reading. What they
    absolutely can tell is whether the content was there. Pages built this way <em>feel</em> faster for
    a simple reason: the part the user knows about (the content) arrives first, and the parts they never
    needed to know about (handlers attaching, client state hydrating) become hidden work that finishes
    quietly in the background.
  </p>

  <h3 id='cls-feel'>Layout shift is perceived slowness</h3>
  <p>
    The third leg of perceived performance is stillness. A page where loading indicators flash in
    and out while regions shift around as each response lands just looks terrible: busy,
    unprofessional, and, crucially, <em>slower</em>, even when the wall-clock time is identical.
    Every spinner and every reflow gives the user a blatant look into the intermediate machinery:
    here is what has not been fetched yet, here is the empty space being held with fallback content,
    here is everything arriving one request at a time, in whatever order the network chose. The user
    is made to watch the kitchen.
  </p>
  <p>
    All of that can simply be swooped up onto the server. Resolve the data there and the
    intermediate steps stop existing on the client: the user sees the full page, ready to go, just
    not yet hydrated, and none of the scaffolding that produced it. Fewer visible steps always reads
    as faster. The mechanics of holding still (containers, honest space reservation, CLS as a field
    metric) get a full chapter in
    <a href='10-containers-content-shifting.html'>§10</a>; what belongs here is the perceptual
    claim: a page that appears at once and never moves feels faster than one that assembles itself
    in front of the user, regardless of what the network tab says.
  </p>

  <h2 id='page-cache'>
    4.4 The cached page: maximized server content becomes a cacheable artifact
  </h2>
  <p>
    There's a compounding payoff hiding in all of this. Once a page is rendered on the server, its
    rendered output, the HTML plus the RSC payload that describes it (<a href='#routing'>§4.5</a>),
    is a concrete <em>artifact</em>. And artifacts can be cached.
  </p>
  <p>
    Next.js does exactly that. Routes that don't depend on request-time information are rendered <strong
      >once</strong
    > (at build time, or on first request, or on a revalidation schedule), and the cached output is served
    to every subsequent visitor without re-rendering: effectively a static site, per route. When part
    of a page
    <em>does</em> need request-time data, the modern model (Partial Prerendering /
    <code>cacheComponents</code>) caches the static shell and streams only the dynamic holes into
    it. The mechanics (<code>"use cache"</code>, revalidation, cache tags) live in
    <a href='15-dedupe-caching.html'>§15</a>; what belongs here is the architectural point:
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Only server-rendered content can participate in this cache.</strong>
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='critical' /> The framework can only cache output it produces; content fetched from the browser
    after hydration is invisible to it: every user re-pays the full cost of every client-side request,
    forever. So each piece of content you move to the server is upgraded twice: it arrives in the first
    paint
    <em>and</em> it becomes eligible to be rendered once and served to everyone. Maximizing the known
    isn't just making one request faster; it's making the page progressively cheaper to serve at all.
    This is also the trap the audit found Tracker in: a client-rendered tree opts out of the entire caching
    layer silently, page by page, with no error to tell you what you gave up.
  </p>

  <h2 id='routing'>4.5 Routing: server routing, client routing, and the RSC payload</h2>
  <p>
    One reasonable worry remains: if the server renders everything, doesn't every click become a
    full page reload: white flash, re-downloaded assets, lost state? This is where the App Router is
    cleverer than classic server rendering, and understanding it requires one piece of vocabulary:
    the <strong>RSC payload</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The RSC payload is a compact, streamable serialization of a rendered server component tree. It
    contains the rendered output of your server components, placeholders marking where client
    components go (plus references to their JS chunks), and any props passed from server to client.
    It is what the server <em>actually produces</em> when it renders; HTML is generated from it for first
    visits. Think of it as "the rendered page, described as data", which is precisely what makes client-side
    merging possible.
  </p>
  <p>Routing then works in two modes:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>First visit: server routing.</strong> The browser requests a URL; the server renders the
      full tree and responds with a complete HTML document (with the RSC payload embedded for hydration).
      Real content paints before JavaScript loads: everything §4.1–§4.3 promised.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Every navigation after: client routing.</strong> Clicking a
      <code>&lt;Link&gt;</code> does <em>not</em> reload the document. The router requests the RSC payload
      for just the route segments that change, and React merges it into the page in place. Shared layouts
      don't re-render and their state survives (<a href='03-mental-model.html#rendering-model'
        >§3.1</a
      >); only the content that differs is replaced. And because
      <code>&lt;Link&gt;</code> prefetches payloads for links in the viewport, the merge often happens
      from cache - navigation lands near-instantly.
    </li>
  </ul>

