  <p>
    This is the answer to the worry, and the reason SSR here doesn't mean "a website from 2005." You
    get server rendering's first paint <em>and</em> an SPA's navigation feel, from the same architecture,
    without building either by hand. But notice it's conditional: the seamless merge works because the
    server renders segments the router can splice. A page that client-fetches everything after mount gets
    neither benefit: its first visit is a shell, and its navigations re-run the whole fetch choreography
    (<a href='13-blocking-requests.html#client'>§13.4</a>).
  </p>

  <h3 id='prefetching'>Links and prefetching</h3>
  <p>
    The instant-navigation story above hangs on one component: <code>&lt;Link&gt;</code>. Internal
    navigation always goes through <code>&lt;Link&gt;</code>, never a plain
    <code>&lt;a&gt;</code>: an anchor tag opts out of the entire model with a full document load:
    layouts torn down, state cleared, scroll reset.
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='critical' /> But <code>&lt;Link&gt;</code> does more than route on the client: it <strong
      >prefetches</strong
    >. When a link enters the viewport (or is hovered), Next.js quietly loads the target route in
    the background, so by the time the user actually clicks, the payload the router needs is usually
    already sitting on the client, and the navigation resolves without waiting on the network at
    all.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/layout.tsx</span><span class="tok-com">{/* Prefetched automatically when the link enters the viewport or is hovered. */}</span>
&lt;<span class="tok-fn">Link</span> <span class="tok-prop">href</span>=<span class="tok-str">"/programs"</span>&gt;Programs&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Link</span>&gt;

<span class="tok-com">{/* A plain anchor opts out of everything: full document load, no prefetch. */}</span>
&lt;a <span class="tok-prop">href</span>=<span class="tok-str">"/contact"</span>&gt;Contact&lt;/a&gt;

<span class="tok-com">{/* Prefetching can be disabled per link: the right call for large lists of
   links (an infinite-scroll table of rows), where eagerly prefetching every
   visible row is wasted work. Prefetch-on-hover is the middle ground. */}</span>
&lt;<span class="tok-fn">Link</span> <span class="tok-prop">prefetch</span>={<span class="tok-kw">false</span>} <span class="tok-prop">href</span>=<span class="tok-str">"/programs/42"</span>&gt;Program 42&lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Link</span>&gt;</pre>
  </Snippet>

  <p>
    How much can be prefetched is, once again, a known/unknown question, this time asked about a
    route the user hasn't visited yet:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Static routes are prefetched in full.</strong> The route was prerendered (<a
        href='#page-cache'>§4.4</a
      >), so its complete payload already exists as a cached artifact; the client can fetch all of
      it ahead of the click and navigate entirely from cache.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Dynamic routes are prefetched partially, or not at all.</strong> The server can't render
      request-time content before the request exists. But if the route has a
      <code>loading.tsx</code>, Next.js prefetches everything that <em>is</em> knowable ahead of time:
      the shared layouts and the loading skeleton. The known travels early; only the unknown waits for
      the click.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3 id='dynamic-loading'>
    The slow-transition trap: dynamic routes without <code>loading.tsx</code>
  </h3>
  <p>
    Follow that second bullet to its failure mode, because the
    <a
      href='https://nextjs.org/docs/app/getting-started/linking-and-navigating#dynamic-routes-without-loadingtsx'
      target='_blank'
      rel='noopener'>docs call it out</a
    >
    as the number-one thing that makes transitions feel slow. A dynamic route with no
    <code>loading.tsx</code> has nothing prefetchable and nothing to show on click, so when the user navigates,
    <em>nothing happens</em> until the server finishes rendering. Click… wait… still the old page. In
    the docs' words, it "can give the users the impression that the app is not responding." Every dynamic
    route should have a
    <code>loading.tsx</code> (or an equivalent Suspense boundary).
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='very' />
  </p>
  <p>
    With the file present, the whole sequence inverts. The layouts and skeleton were partially
    prefetched, so the navigation <strong>commits immediately</strong>: the URL changes, the known
    structure paints on click, and the server's content streams into the reserved fallback when it
    arrives (<a href='11-suspense-streaming.html#streaming'>§11.2</a>). The docs enumerate the
    benefits: immediate visual feedback, shared layouts that stay interactive while the route loads
    (the user can even interrupt the navigation and click elsewhere), better TTFB/FCP/TTI. But
    notice what the pattern <em>is</em>: contain-the-unknown, applied to navigation. The known part
    of the next page travels ahead of the click; the unknown part streams in behind it, into space
    that was reserved for it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Three edge notes close the loop. If a dynamic segment's pages are enumerable ahead of time, <code
      >generateStaticParams</code
    > promotes them to prerendered static routes: full prefetch, no fallback needed at all. On slow networks
    the prefetch may not finish before the click; <code>useLinkStatus</code> exposes a <code
      >pending</code
    > flag for an immediate, debounced hint on the link itself. And prefetching can't start until
    <code>&lt;Link&gt;</code> hydrates (it is a client component), which is one more reason to keep first-render
    JavaScript small (<a href='07-first-render-js.html'>§7</a>).
  </p>

